\u2500\u2500 STORYBOARD \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500
The story follows the Apollo 13 crisis, framed as a survival thriller. The hook is the sheer isolation and sudden failure: 200,000 miles from home with a dead ship. The narrative beats escalate the danger from a general explosion to the specific, lethal problem of CO2 buildup. The emotional arc moves from shock (the explosion), to rising dread (the poisoned air), to a tense intellectual puzzle (the square peg/round hole problem), to a moment of pure human ingenuity (the makeshift filter), and finally to profound relief and triumph (the splashdown). The clip strategy relies on specific, iconic moments from the mission's archival footage. Real footage exists for Mission Control's reaction, the engineers solving the filter problem on the ground, and the splashdown. We will use the same search query for the engineering beat across two screens to show the problem and then the solution from the same source video. The story will be told through timestamped clips, not ambient playback, as the source footage is extensive.
